## 987

These are constant expressions: their real domains have no variable restrictions, and every denominator is nonzero as shown below.

### a

The conjugate denominator product is
$(11-2\sqrt{30})(11+2\sqrt{30})=121-120=1$.
Thus neither denominator vanishes, and

$$
\frac1{11-2\sqrt{30}}-\frac1{11+2\sqrt{30}}
=\frac{(11+2\sqrt{30})-(11-2\sqrt{30})}{1}
=4\sqrt{30}.
$$

### b

$(3+2\sqrt2)(3-2\sqrt2)=9-8=1$, so

$$
\frac5{3+2\sqrt2}+\frac5{3-2\sqrt2}
=\frac{5[(3-2\sqrt2)+(3+2\sqrt2)]}{1}
=30.
$$

### c

$(\sqrt5+\sqrt3)(\sqrt5-\sqrt3)=5-3=2$, so

$$
\frac{\sqrt5-\sqrt3}{\sqrt5+\sqrt3}
+\frac{\sqrt5+\sqrt3}{\sqrt5-\sqrt3}
=\frac{(\sqrt5-\sqrt3)^2+(\sqrt5+\sqrt3)^2}{2}
=\frac{2(5+3)}2=8.
$$

### d

$(11-\sqrt{21})(11+\sqrt{21})=121-21=100$, so

$$
\frac{11+\sqrt{21}}{11-\sqrt{21}}
+\frac{11-\sqrt{21}}{11+\sqrt{21}}
=\frac{(11+\sqrt{21})^2+(11-\sqrt{21})^2}{100}
=\frac{2(121+21)}{100}=\frac{71}{25}.
$$

## 988

Both identities are constant real expressions. Their denominators are squares of positive numbers.

### a

$$
(2+\sqrt3)^2-1=6+4\sqrt3
=\sqrt3(4+2\sqrt3)=\sqrt3(\sqrt3+1)^2.
$$

Since $(\sqrt3+1)^2>0$, division proves the stated identity.

### b

$$
(3+\sqrt5)^2-4=10+6\sqrt5
=\sqrt5(6+2\sqrt5)=\sqrt5(\sqrt5+1)^2.
$$

Since $(\sqrt5+1)^2>0$, division proves the stated identity.

## 989

### a: Simplify and Evaluate

The original real domain is exactly $a\ge0,\ b>0$. The individual roots require $a,b\ge0$; under these restrictions $\sqrt{ab}+2b$ vanishes exactly when $b=0$.

On this full original domain, $\sqrt{ab}=\sqrt a\sqrt b$, and

$$
\frac{(\sqrt a+\sqrt b)^2-b}{\sqrt{ab}+2b}
=\frac{\sqrt a(\sqrt a+2\sqrt b)}{\sqrt b(\sqrt a+2\sqrt b)}
=\frac{\sqrt a}{\sqrt b}=\sqrt{\frac ab}.
$$

The canceled factor is strictly positive because $b>0$. At $a=5,\ b=2$ the exact value is

$$
\sqrt{\frac52}=\frac{\sqrt{10}}2.
$$

### b: Simplify and Evaluate

The original real domain is exactly $x>0,\ y\ge0$. The individual roots require $x,y\ge0$; the denominator $x+\sqrt{xy}$ is zero exactly when $x=0$.

On this full original domain,

$$
\frac{(\sqrt x-\sqrt y)^2+4\sqrt{xy}}{x+\sqrt{xy}}
=\frac{x+y+2\sqrt{xy}}{\sqrt x(\sqrt x+\sqrt y)}
=\frac{(\sqrt x+\sqrt y)^2}{\sqrt x(\sqrt x+\sqrt y)}
=1+\sqrt{\frac yx}.
$$

Both $\sqrt x$ and the canceled factor $\sqrt x+\sqrt y$ are strictly positive. At $x=0.4=2/5,\ y=0.6=3/5$ the exact value is

$$
1+\sqrt{\frac32}=1+\frac{\sqrt6}{2}.
$$

## 990

### a

Use the stipulated domain $a\ge1$. Put $s=\sqrt{a^2-1}\ge0$. Since $(a-s)(a+s)=1$ and $a+s>0$, also $a-s=1/(a+s)>0$; the original denominator cannot vanish.

$$
\sqrt{\frac{a+s}{a-s}}
=\sqrt{(a+s)^2}
=|a+s|=a+s
=a+\sqrt{a^2-1}.
$$

This includes $a=1$, where the value is 1. For completeness, without the stipulation the expression's full natural real domain would be $(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)$, because the inner root requires $a^2\ge1$, the conjugate product is 1, and the quotient equals a positive square. On that larger domain the result is $|a+\sqrt{a^2-1}|$, not unconditionally $a+\sqrt{a^2-1}$.

### b

The full natural real domain is all $b\in\mathbb R$. Put $s=\sqrt{b^2+1}$. Then $s>|b|$, so both $s-b$ and the original denominator $s+b$ are strictly positive. Since $(s-b)(s+b)=1$,

$$
\sqrt{\frac{s-b}{s+b}}
=\sqrt{(s-b)^2}
=|s-b|=s-b
=\sqrt{b^2+1}-b.
$$

## 991

These are constant real identities with no denominators.

### a

$(2\sqrt3-1)^2=13-4\sqrt3$. Also $\sqrt3>1$, so $2\sqrt3-1>0$. Therefore the radicand is positive and its principal square root is

$$
\sqrt{13-4\sqrt3}=|2\sqrt3-1|=2\sqrt3-1.
$$

### b

Likewise, $(2\sqrt3+1)^2=13+4\sqrt3$ with $2\sqrt3+1>0$. Both radicands are positive, so

$$
\sqrt{13+4\sqrt3}\sqrt{13-4\sqrt3}
=(2\sqrt3+1)(2\sqrt3-1)=12-1=11.
$$

## 992: Exact Comparisons Without Root Tables

Each comparison below uses only exact arithmetic. Both quantities being squared are positive, so squaring preserves and reflects their order.

### a

$3>9/4$ implies $\sqrt3>3/2$. Hence
$(1+\sqrt3)^2=4+2\sqrt3>7=(\sqrt7)^2$, and

$$
\sqrt7<1+\sqrt3.
$$

### b

$6-\sqrt3>0$ because $36>3$. Also $27>25$ implies $3\sqrt3>5$, so
$(6-\sqrt3)^2-19=20-12\sqrt3=4(5-3\sqrt3)<0$. Thus

$$
\sqrt{19}>6-\sqrt3.
$$

### c

$$
\left(\frac{\sqrt{10}+\sqrt{14}}2\right)^2
=\frac{24+2\sqrt{140}}4=6+\sqrt{35}<12
$$

because $35<36$. Therefore

$$
\frac{\sqrt{10}+\sqrt{14}}2<\sqrt{12}.
$$

The denominator is the nonzero constant 2.

### d

$(2\sqrt2+3\sqrt3)^2=35+12\sqrt6$. Since
$(12\sqrt6)^2=864>841=29^2$ and both terms are positive, $12\sqrt6>29$. Therefore the squared left side exceeds $35+29=64$, and

$$
2\sqrt2+3\sqrt3>8.
$$

## 993: Proofs and All Equality Cases

The stipulated domain for all four subparts is exactly $a>0,\ b>0$. All displayed roots are real and positive. All denominators $2$, $a+b$, $a$, $b$, $\sqrt a$, and $\sqrt b$ that occur are nonzero. Each inequality becomes an equality if and only if $a=b$.

### a

Both sides are positive, and their squares differ by

$$
\frac{a^2+b^2}{2}-\left(\frac{a+b}{2}\right)^2
=\frac{(a-b)^2}{4}\ge0.
$$

Taking principal roots proves the inequality. The difference is zero exactly when $a=b$.

### b

Both sides are positive, and their squares differ by

$$
\frac{a^2+b^2}{2}-ab=\frac{(a-b)^2}{2}\ge0.
$$

Taking principal roots proves the inequality, with equality exactly when $a=b$.

### c

On the stipulated domain,

$$
\sqrt{ab}-\frac{2ab}{a+b}
=\frac{\sqrt{ab}}{a+b}(a+b-2\sqrt{ab})
=\frac{\sqrt{ab}}{a+b}(\sqrt a-\sqrt b)^2\ge0.
$$

The prefactor is strictly positive, so equality holds exactly when $\sqrt a=\sqrt b$, equivalently $a=b$.

### d

Because $a,b>0$, $\sqrt{a^2/b}=a/\sqrt b$ and $\sqrt{b^2/a}=b/\sqrt a$. Write $u=\sqrt a>0,\ v=\sqrt b>0$. Then

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{\frac{a^2}{b}}+\sqrt{\frac{b^2}{a}}-\sqrt a-\sqrt b
&=\frac{u^2}{v}+\frac{v^2}{u}-u-v\\
&=\frac{u^3+v^3-u^2v-uv^2}{uv}\\
&=\frac{(u+v)(u-v)^2}{uv}\ge0.
\end{aligned}
$$

Here $(u+v)/(uv)>0$, so equality holds exactly when $u=v$, equivalently $a=b$.

## 994: Existence

Both original expressions have the full natural domain $x\in\mathbb R$ and no denominators.

### a

$\sqrt{(x-5)^2}=|x-5|$. If $x\ge5$, the left side $|x-5|-x=-5$, which cannot equal 25. If $x<5$, it is $5-2x$, and

$$
5-2x=25\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x=-10.
$$

This satisfies the branch condition. Direct exact substitution gives $\sqrt{(-15)^2}-(-10)=15+10=25$. Such a value exists, and the complete solution set is $\{-10\}$.

### b

$\sqrt{(x-1)^2}=|x-1|$. If $x\ge1$, $x-|x-1|=1$. If $x<1$, $x-|x-1|=2x-1<1$. Thus the entire range is $(-\infty,1]$; it does not contain 9. No real value exists, and the solution set is empty.

